 and this includes the effects of temperature which modifies the coeffcients.
 The first term is a Dirac delta function that adjusts the mean absorption.
 Refinement input should provide an estimate
 of the incident beam intensity for normalisation so that the true magnitude of 
 absorption
 can be more accurately incorporated.
 The masks for refining each coefficient are either 0 (do not refine) or 1 (refi
 ne),
 except in the case of coefficient C which can either be set to equal coefficien
 t B
 at all times (use -1) or set to an independent unrefined value (use 0) or can b
 e
 independently refined (use 1).
 SPECIES   1 A:   0.000000 MASKA:   0 B:   1.000000 MASKB:   1 C:   1.000000 MASKC:   1
 DEBYE-WALLER FACTORS USED FOR EACH REGION (ATOM TYPE 1,......, ATOM TYPE N)
 MASKS FOR REFINEMENT OF D-W FACTORS:
 0.860000
  1
 To account for free surface and solute effects on the Debye-Waller factor, the free surface type and binding  equation coefficients are given for each region.  The free surface type is given first (0 = no free surface, 1 = free  surface at the bottom of the region and 2 = free surface at the top of the region) and then the coefficients A, B and C  where DW = DW0/BINDING, where BINDING was determined using a force field model specially programmed for FCC with   facetting in any hkl.  BINDING=1.00000-1.38455*[vac]-(A-B*[vac])*Exp(-C(layer-1)^0.9082) with DW being the applied	Debye-Waller factor and DW0 being the bulk Debye-Waller factor: 
  0  0.000000000  0.000000000  1.000000000
 END DEBYE-WALLER REFINEMENT DETAILS (SEE ABOVE)
